WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.301
I want you to take a look

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.503
at this number behind me.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:04.671
1 billion.

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:08.174
428,600,000.

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.577
This is India's projected population

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:11.678
by mid 20,

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.146
23, almost 3 million

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.715
more than what China is expected to be.

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:17.784
And that means that in the coming

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.753
months, India will overtake China

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:21.054
as the most populated

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:22.922
populous nation in the world,

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.324
according to the United Nations.

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:25.959
But that explosion of people

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.527
is driving competition

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.763
for the most sought after jobs in India.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.464
And many are struggling to keep up.

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:35.335
Vedika Sud has more from New Delhi

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.470
In a village outside New Delhi, a

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.140
doting mother attends to her little girl.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:46.713
Mahi is just two days old.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.748
Her cries mark a symbolic moment.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:52.352
India is expected to surpass

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.254
China as the world's most

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:56.122
populous country. I don't see

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.491
my father

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.193
Veit-wild seeing and mother Lakshmi,

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.594
our school dropouts

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:03.296
Their families had little income

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.399
and many children to feed

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.602
Seeing a farmer started

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.504
working with his father in their fields

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:12.639
at a young age,

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.441
he says he'll do whatever it takes

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:16.709
to help me achieve her dreams.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.813
even if I have to sell my field

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.481
to educate her, I will.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.949
One of the biggest challenges

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.717
she'll have to face is the country's

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.286
growing population.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:26.853
There's stiff

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:29.923
competition, Singh tells me.

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.225
India's birth rate has slowed,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.793
but the country is still quickly

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.929
adding to its 1.4 billion

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.864
strong population.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:38.998
According to UNICEF,

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:42.902
more than 67,300 babies are born in India

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:43.937
every day.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:44.370
That's one

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.073
sixth of the world's birth counts daily.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.910
Already, more than half of all Indians

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.645
are under the age of 30.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.913
That means a huge potential

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.815
to grow the national economy.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.717
But education and investment

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:58.918
need to keep up.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.320
If there are going to be jobs

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.888
for a new generation,

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.590
I really feel it's a ten year

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.758
opportunity to do it right.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:07.894
And this is a dividend, do it wrong.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.528
And this is going

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.163
to be extremely worrying

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:11.664
for the Indian economy.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:12.832
India's youngsters.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.268
28 year old Sunil

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:15.902
Kumar has a

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:17.137
postgraduate degree

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:20.406
but doesn't hold a steady job.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.308
For years now, he's been sweeping

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.344
the floors of a school in his village

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:25.712
in Haryana. State.

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:26.846
Just give the water

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.548
and doubling up as a tutor

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.416
for young students.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.951
Despite his education, Kumar

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:32.952
barely makes enough

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.621
to support his ailing father,

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:37.390
and the rest of his family.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.559
Sunil says it angers him

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:40.827
that he doesn't have a steady

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:41.694
job, despite

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.963
his educational qualifications

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:44.731
in the air.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.767
Across India, the most highly sought

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:48.635
after jobs

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:51.137
are more competitive than ever before

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.605
pursuing their dreams.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.240
Tens of thousands of students

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.276
from small towns move to big cities

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.143
to be called

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.679
for the coveted civil services exam.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:02.248
Some lecturing, others

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:03.583
all have been trying to crack

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.285
the exam for four years now.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:08.354
El-Gamal

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.455
says his younger brother

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:11.491
is sponsoring his education.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.293
They could have bought three to four cars

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.761
with the money they've spent on me,

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:15.962
he says.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:18.431
Over 1 million people

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:20.800
sit for the entrance test each year.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.136
Less than 1% make the cut

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:24.604
The population is growing

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:25.471
and the working age

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.173
population is growing.

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.475
If that category of people

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.711
do not find enough employment,

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.781
then what was meant to be an opportunity?

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:36.516
The bulge in that demographic

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:38.318
dividend, so-called,

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.353
could become a huge challenge

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.889
and problem for India.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.925
India's new global title will mean little

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:49.062
if it doesn't come with fresh opportunity

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:50.196
Vedika Sud

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:52.665
CNN, New Delhi.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.201
All those figures are from the UN States

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.803
of the Population Report.

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:58.938
Here are some of the headline numbers.

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:00.540
The world's population is now

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.175
8 billion strong.

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.943
That's largely a result of global

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:07.814
life expectancy increasing to 72.8 years.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:10.984
That is nine years higher than 1990.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.218
But in Europe,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.320
a population is expected to decline

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.423
7% between now and 2050.

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:19.792
Those numbers raise important questions.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.661
Are they too many of us

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.096
or is population decline

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.597
an existential threat?

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.632
Will all children

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:27.233
contribute to climate change,

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:30.003
and how much will they suffer from it?

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:31.671
Governments have grappled

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:32.972
with these issues

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.007
for generations,

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:35.508
even implementing policies

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:37.543
to try to find the answers.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.345
But as the UN Population

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.281
Fund director Natalia Canan puts

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:41.848
it, human

00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:42.949
reproduction

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.418
is neither the problem nor the solution.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.820
When we put gender equality

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.389
and the rights at the heart

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:51.291
of our population, policies

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:52.458
we are stronger,

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.394
more resilient and better able

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:55.561
to deal with the challenges

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:57.196
resulting from rapidly

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.631
changing populations.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:04:59.098
And Dr.

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:02.435
Natalia joins me now live from Brussels.

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.804
Great to have you on the show.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:05.104
You know,

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:07.273
these numbers could be quite scary,

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.141
but as you say,

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:09.509
we've got to put gender equality

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.512
at the heart of this and the numbers.

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:13.246
Perhaps it paints

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:14.280
another picture for me,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.448
sort of a fuller picture

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:17.216
that around 24% of women

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:17.750
and girls

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.353
on able to say no to sex while 11%

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.555
unable to make decisions

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:23.923
over contraception.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.059
And I'm curious to what extent

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:27.360
are the rising population

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.995
numbers in certain geographies

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.764
because it's not across the board

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:35.568
correlated to the data around the rights

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:36.135
of women.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.204
Well, you know

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.173
it's such a great question Eleanor.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.775
I'm very happy to be here because

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:44.911
we have to radically rethink

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:47.146
the way that we look at population

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:50.083
and population policy today.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.586
Two thirds of us live in places

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.455
where population

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:57.190
is already trending

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.225
below replacement rate.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.259
And yes,

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.295
for one third of the globe,

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:04.397
population continues to increase.

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:06.332
But we're at a time when there's great

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:08.134
geographic diversity.

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.736
In some places,

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:11.671
the average age of the population

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:12.805
is 50 years

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:16.242
old, whereas in other countries it's 15.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:18.811
So when you have this kind of a situation

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.380
of changing dynamics,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.081
you have to take things

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:25.118
through the lens of the woman

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:27.253
who is going to bear the child.

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:30.056
Her decision about her fertility

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:31.958
is an individual decision,

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:32.592
and the woman

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.627
and the couple have the right

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:35.895
to actually decide the number

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.797
and spacing and timing of their children.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.298
So we want to refocus

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:42.135
the question of population into

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:44.103
what are we doing

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:45.838
to make that choice a reality?

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:46.672
Because today it's

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:48.307
not a reality everywhere,

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:50.510
not just in high fertility countries,

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.044
but also in those that are facing

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.045
population decline.

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:56.482
And really

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:57.917
fascinating

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.652
look, we've just spoken about India.

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:01.120
We had a fantastic insight

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.257
into what the experiences with regards

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:05.958
to a young demographic,

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:08.094
so much excitement about what that means.

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:09.028
But there needs to be

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.696
a convergence, right?

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:12.532
With population growth,

00:07:12.532 --> 00:07:14.567
this is what we see on education

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:16.402
resources, job opportunities.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:18.304
Because if it isn't a convergence,

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.505
that is when you're going to be

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:21.174
dealing with,

00:07:21.174 --> 00:07:21.707
you know,

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:22.241
perhaps

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:23.709
an unstable future

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:26.012
for the new generation.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:26.779
Well, you know what?

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:27.079
Again,

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.282
I think this is a very important moment

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:30.583
to look at the dynamics

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:32.685
and to put the facts first.

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:34.320
The issue of young people's

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.322
education is relevant

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:37.757
in every single country,

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:40.059
not just countries with high fertility.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:40.726
But, you know, I'm

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.195
sitting in Brussels today.

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:43.129
Europe is worried

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.264
about the fate of young people

00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:47.266
and the fact that relative

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:49.569
to an aging population,

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:52.472
the labor force is changing dramatically.

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:54.907
So equipping every young person

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.310
and as the India segment showed,

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.545
the girl child in particular,

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:01.047
to be able to succeed,

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:03.449
she's going to need her education.

00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:05.852
So this is very fundamental.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:07.820
I think the other point is that

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.890
for places where

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.259
there is high fertility,

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:14.594
if this is the woman's choice,

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:15.661
that's fine.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.230
But in much of the world,

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:18.498
women are asking

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:20.566
for contraceptive choice.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.035
And have we been able to fulfill that?

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:23.569
Well, today,

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:25.571
that answer is a resounding no.

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:27.340
So we've got to look at all the factors

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.942
and not point fingers at the woman

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:31.811
as either.

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.412
The reason for high fertility

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:34.647
or low fertility

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:36.716
But understand her perspective as to

00:08:36.916 --> 00:08:38.417
how does she want to organize this.

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:39.118
Fascinating.

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.920
Because because it has been

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.155
it has been centered around women,

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:43.723
I think

00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:45.791
particularly with these

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:47.326
low fertility rates.

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:47.994
As you say

00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:49.262
I mean since the 1950s

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:50.263
and this is what really surprised

00:08:50.263 --> 00:08:51.497
me, the average number of children

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:53.299
women are having

00:08:53.299 --> 00:08:55.501
has halved from five to 2.3.

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.169
And we've even reached a point

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.704
where wealthier nations

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:01.541
are starting to incentivize families

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:03.976
or women to have more children

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.645
because they're worried about population.

00:09:05.645 --> 00:09:06.846
But as a consequence of issues

00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:09.482
that women are choosing not to have kids.

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:12.251
And then here's the issue.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:12.451
You know,

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:13.953
are we worried about

00:09:13.953 --> 00:09:15.187
too many people on the planet

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:17.490
or we worried about population decline?

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:19.625
Well, you know what?

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:23.696
We in UNFPA are very focused on the point

00:09:23.696 --> 00:09:25.965
that you've got to ask a better question

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:27.600
if you're looking for a solution.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.101
So if you're looking for a solution

00:09:29.101 --> 00:09:30.303
for climate change,

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:32.972
fertility is not going to be the answer.

00:09:33.306 --> 00:09:35.107
Similarly, I think

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:36.776
you raise a very important issue

00:09:36.776 --> 00:09:38.344
about the variety

00:09:38.344 --> 00:09:41.647
of total fertility rates in countries.

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:45.651
And the question as is, is the woman

00:09:46.085 --> 00:09:49.088
who is having one child or two children,

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:50.890
which would be replacement rate?

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.925
Is she making a rational decision

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:55.995
and is she going to be incentivized

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:57.363
by a bonus

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:58.531
if she has a second, third

00:09:58.531 --> 00:09:59.565
or fourth child?

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:01.500
Well, you know, women are pretty smart

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:02.501
and they are looking

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:03.903
at how to invest in the children

00:10:03.903 --> 00:10:05.371
that they already have.

00:10:05.371 --> 00:10:07.740
And they're also looking at inequality

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.277
because women are bearing the burden

00:10:11.377 --> 00:10:13.546
of not only looking after children,

00:10:13.546 --> 00:10:16.816
but actually over the elderly generation

00:10:16.816 --> 00:10:17.283
as well.

00:10:18.384 --> 00:10:19.919
And simultaneously,

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:20.686
older people

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:23.456
who would love to be involved

00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.091
are not being induced

00:10:25.091 --> 00:10:25.625
to be able

00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:26.359
to do

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:27.360
the types of work

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.461
and the types of childcare

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:30.029
that they might actually enjoy.

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:31.530
So we've got to look at the big picture

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.466
by asking the real question

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:35.267
and not by trying to tell women

00:10:35.267 --> 00:10:37.036
have this exact amount of children

00:10:37.036 --> 00:10:38.971
because we want a supposedly

00:10:38.971 --> 00:10:40.439
ideal population size.

00:10:40.439 --> 00:10:41.240
It's not going to work.

